// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4): "Every
// potentially-evaluated call to it shall produce a constant expression. If it
// does not, the program is ill-formed." The implicit object argument is part
// of the call, so a receiver that is not usable in a constant expression
// makes the call ill-formed even when every explicit argument is a constant:
// `c` here is an ordinary non-`constexpr` local, and reading it is not a
// constant expression ([expr.const.core]).
//
// This is the receiver-side counterpart of
// consteval_call_with_non_constant_argument_is_rejected.scpp. Compare
// consteval_member_function_call_on_named_object_folds.scpp, which is the
// same program with `constexpr` on the receiver and is accepted.
//
// Rejection independently confirmed with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which
// reports: "error: call to consteval function 'Counter::doubled' is not a
// constant expression" / "note: read of non-constexpr variable 'c' is not
// allowed in a constant expression".
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS the .stderr pin. scpp does reject
// the program, but with "error: internal error: no generated code for
// resolved function 'Counter_doubled' called here" -- an internal-compiler-
// error report, not a diagnosis of §9.1(4). Do not relax the pin to admit
// `internal error`.
class Counter {
public:
    virtual ~Counter() = default;
public:
    int value{};

    constexpr Counter(int v) {
        this->value = v;
        return;
    }

    consteval int doubled() const {
        return this->value * 2;
    }
};

int main() {
    Counter c{21};
    return c.doubled();
}
